    "type": "question",
    "label": "26",
    "prompt": "Let $P(x)=x^3+ax^2+bx+6$, which has factor $x-1$. When $P(x)$ is divided by $x+2$, the remainder is $-6$. Find $a$ and $b$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=-3,\\quad b=-4$.",
      "worked": "Since $x-1$ is a factor, $P(1)=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1+a+b+6\\\\a+b&=-7\\end{align*}$$\nBy the remainder theorem, $P(-2)=-6$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(-2)&=-8+4a-2b+6\\\\4a-2b-2&=-6\\\\4a-2b&=-4\\\\2a-b&=-2\\end{align*}$$\nAdding $a+b=-7$ and $2a-b=-2$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}3a&=-9\\\\a&=-3\\\\b&=-7-(-3)\\\\b&=-4\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 108
  },
